    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 4 USER]\ne030\tYou are assuming we can identify a minimal stable format.\ne031\tPriya, who designed the data layer, says that doing so before seeing stranger collections would formalize the wrong abstractions.\ne032\tShe wants public examples precisely to learn what should be stable.\ne033\tOur senior maintainer says that is using users to discover architecture at their expense.\ne034\tThey respect each other, but the conversation has become “learning” versus “responsibility,” and both words are doing too much work.\n[Turn 4 ASSISTANT]\ne035\tThose positions may differ on who bears the cost of uncertainty.\ne036\tPriya expects real use to reveal the right abstractions; the maintainer expects early users to absorb migration and support costs.\ne037\tA release design could make that cost visible and bounded—for example, limiting the beta to disposable projects or providing an export path—yet either choice may reduce the natural use you wanted to observe.\ne038\tThere may not be a way to learn from committed use without creating some obligation to those users.\n[Turn 5 USER]\ne039\tWe learned today that installation is easier than we thought for people using the common package manager, but one private lab had a staff engineer quietly solving problems for everyone.\ne040\tTheir researchers reported a smooth experience because they never saw the setup work.\ne041\tThat makes our private evidence look weaker.\ne042\tOn the other hand, a volunteer wrote a new installer over the weekend and wants real users before polishing it.\ne043\tI cannot tell whether this argues for release or for another round of testing.\n[Turn 5 ASSISTANT]\ne044\tIt argues against treating the private labs as representative, but it does not choose the next step by itself.\ne045\tThe new installer could change the support burden, and public use could test it under real conditions.\ne046\tA bounded installation test with people who lack staff engineering might answer that question faster than either a full beta or more internal polishing.\ne047\tStill, if every uncertainty becomes a separate pre-release study, you recreate the endless preview problem you already identified.",
